---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Quick start" current="/guides/quick-start/">
  <article class="doc">
    <header class="doc-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">Guide</span>
      <h1>Quick start</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Get up and running in a few minutes: set up a config, run your first
        validation, and pick the output format that pays off for your workflow.
      </p>
    </header>

    <h2>1. Create a config</h2>
    <pre><code>dollarlint init</code></pre>
    <p>
      In a terminal, the interactive setup writes a starter <code
        >.dollarlint.toml</code
      >. It asks before overwriting an existing config, and <code
        >--defaults</code
      >
      skips prompts for automation. In non-interactive runs, DollarLint uses the same
      defaults and prints a notice unless <code>--defaults</code> is set.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Validate a directory</h2>
    <pre><code>dollarlint validate .</code></pre>
    <p>
      DollarLint discovers JSON, JSONC, JSON5, JSON Lines, YAML, and TOML files,
      skips common dependency and cache directories, respects <code
        >.gitignore</code
      > files as it descends, loads declared schemas, and groups results by file.
    </p>

    <div class="note">
      <p>
        Files without an in-file schema, config association, built-in
        association, or catalog match are skipped by default, but still counted
        in the summary so discovery remains visible. <code
          >.dollarlint.toml</code
        > is covered automatically by DollarLint's embedded config schema. Set <code
          >schemas.requireCoverage = true</code
        > when every included file must be covered by one of those schema sources.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>3. Prepare whole-repository runs</h2>
    <p>
      Some projects keep schemas in installed dependencies, such as <code
        >node_modules</code
      >. If configs use local <code>$schema</code> paths into those dependencies,
      install the project dependencies before validating so DollarLint sees the same
      schema context as the project tools.
    </p>
    <p>
      Full-repository runs also discover test fixtures, snapshots, generated
      baselines, and partial config fragments. Keep those visible when you want
      a broad audit, or exclude them narrowly when you want application config
      only.
    </p>
    <pre><code>dollarlint validate . \
  --exclude 'testdata/**' \
  --exclude 'fixtures/**' \
  --exclude '**/*.snapshot.json'</code></pre>

    <h2>4. Pinpoint issues with locations</h2>
    <pre><code>dollarlint validate ./config --locations</code></pre>
    <p>
      Line and column positions are available for text output with <code
        >--locations</code
      >. JSON and SARIF request source mapping automatically so scripts and code
      scanning tools can point at the closest source location.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Cash out machine-readable output</h2>
    <pre><code>dollarlint validate . --format json
dollarlint validate . --format sarif --output dollarlint.sarif</code></pre>
    <p>
      Text is the default. JSON and SARIF are requested per run with <code
        >--format</code
      >; output files are requested per run with <code>--output</code>.
    </p>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>
